## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by **reference**.

```jsx
// Re-renders every time Parent renders — new object reference each time
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Even if the values are identical, `{ color: 'red' } !== { color: 'red' }` — they're different objects in memory. So `Child` re-renders even when nothing meaningful changed.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | Component output | Child re-renders with same props |
| `useMemo` | Computed value | Expensive calculation, or stable object/array reference |
| `useCallback` | Function reference | Passing callbacks to memoized children |

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component so it skips re-render if props are shallowly equal:
```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, value }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{value}</button>);
```

**`useCallback`** — stabilizes a function reference across renders:
```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — stabilizes an object/array reference or caches an expensive result:
```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark', size: 'lg' }), []);
```

> Don't reach for these by default. Memoization has overhead. Profile first, optimize second.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every component that calls `useContext(MyContext)` re-renders when the context **value** changes. The trap: inline object literals always produce a new reference.

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every render, ALL consumers re-render
<MyContext.Provider value={{ user, logout }}>
```

```jsx
// Good — stable reference, consumers only re-render when user/logout change
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, logout }), [user, logout]);
<MyContext.Provider value={value}>
```

Another pattern: **split contexts** by update frequency. Put fast-changing state in one context and stable config in another so stable consumers aren't dragged along.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent's input:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const items = ['Apple', 'Banana', 'Cherry'];

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}                          // new array ref each render
        onSelect={(item) => console.log(item)} // new function ref each render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  // renders even when items/onSelect haven't meaningfully changed
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` is stable; only re-renders when `items` or `onSelect` actually change:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

  const items = useMemo(() => ['Apple', 'Banana', 'Cherry'], []); // stable ref

  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => {
    console.log(item);
  }, []); // stable ref

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(i => <div key={i} onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
});
```

**Result:** typing in the input no longer causes `ExpensiveList` to re-render at all.

---

### Key heuristic

`React.memo` is the gate — it prevents re-renders. `useCallback`/`useMemo` are the keys — they keep props passing through that gate stable. You need both sides for the optimization to work.
